**Whole task:** register a Eurorack skiff in the robot's frame, then press a button and turn a knob to a set position.

**Our design.** The PAROL6 manual stops at pick-and-place. From here on the plan is ours, built on the [Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/) and the [end-effector docs](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/).

## Parts

- The skiff. See [the skiff target](/lore/skiff-target); the choice is open.
- A rigid shared base plate for the arm and the skiff. The robot's base mounts on 30 by 30 mm profile ([Mounting](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/mounting/)).
- A knob-turning end effector: a printed knob socket or a TPU-padded finger.

<TimelapseLinks milestone="m5-touch-the-skiff" />

## Concepts

- **Coordinate frames and registration.** Touch three fiducials on the skiff panel to find its pose in the robot's frame ([Reference frames](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/theory/reference-frames/)).
- **Eurorack mechanics.** Rails, HP (5.08 mm, from [Doepfer's A-100 construction details](https://www.doepfer.de/a100_man/a100m_e.htm)), and the jack and knob geometry of each module.
- **Why compliance matters** when a rigid arm touches something rigid.

**needs_source:** the jack and knob positions of each module, measured from the real panels.

## How-to

- Python API motion, the tool frame and slow approach moves ([Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/)).
- Our registration script, written as a completion task.

## Practice

- Caliper measurement of the panel geometry.
- Touch-off drills.
- Tuning the approach speed.

## Done check

With the skiff at a fresh pose, Joel registers it in under 5 minutes. Then the arm turns a named knob from fully counter-clockwise to noon, measured by the voltage on a CV output, and presses a button 10 times out of 10.

## Terms

- **registration**: finding where an object sits in the robot's frame by touching known points on it. This is our design, built on the frames in [Reference frames](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/theory/reference-frames/).
- **fiducial**: a known reference point on the skiff panel that the tool touches during registration. Our design.
- **compliance**: give in the tool, so that a small position error does not become a large force. Our design, needed because open-loop steppers cannot feel force ([Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).

